# Inkling 2 Small on purpose-built silicon

A hardware-architecture study by Aercrist. It asks what a chip built around
[Inkling 2 Small](https://huggingface.co/thinkingmachines/Inkling-2-Small) (594B parameters, 17B
active per token) would deliver against the best published GPU deployment, which memory architecture
gets there, what keeping the weights reloadable costs, and how the answer changes for smaller models.
It is the sequel to our [DeepSeek-V4.1 study](https://github.com/Aercrist/dsv41-accel-study) and
follows its rules: every number is rendered from `results/*.json`, every constant is sourced or swept,
and the predictions were written down before anything was simulated.

This study is independent. It is not affiliated with or endorsed by Thinking Machines Lab.

**Nothing here was fabricated or measured on hardware by us.** The GPU figures reproduce other people's published
measurements; the chips are designs on paper; one circuit block was synthesized.

## Findings

Against B300 GPUs running today's best published engines, in the best layout for each metric (2x, 4x or
8x B300), for a chip that
keeps every weight in reloadable on-die memory. Median and range are over {{ MC.n }} joint draws of
{{ U.params | length }} uncertain inputs:

| | median | 5th to 95th percentile | central case | confidence |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **speed for one user** | **{{ x(MC.speed_batch1.median) }}** | {{ x(MC.speed_batch1.p05) }} to {{ x(MC.speed_batch1.p95) }} | {{ x(RS.speed_batch1) }} ({{ n(s.batch1.tok_s_user, -2) }} against {{ n(g4.batch1.tok_s_user, -1) }} tok/s) | medium: the GPU side is calibrated; the chip side takes its time per operation from a published machine |
| **tokens per joule**, decode, at each platform's best interactive point | **{{ x(MC.tokens_per_joule_best.median) }}** | {{ x(MC.tokens_per_joule_best.p05) }} to {{ x(MC.tokens_per_joule_best.p95) }} | {{ x(RS.tokens_per_joule_best) }} | medium |
| **cost per token**, decode, at the same point | **{{ x(MC.cost_best.median, 2) }}** | {{ x(MC.cost_best.p05, 2) }} to {{ x(MC.cost_best.p95, 2) }} | {{ x(RS.cost_best) }} | low: rests on token demand, lifetime and margin |
| **what reloadable weights cost** against fixing them in the masks | | | {{ x(I.designs.s_over_f.dies) }} the silicon; speed not separable: the two designs' speeds come from two different published machines | medium on silicon: the fixed-weight side scales one shipped 8B chip |

1. **On-chip weights buy speed and energy, not cheap tokens.** One user gets about
   {{ n(s.batch1.tok_s_user, -2) }} tokens a second instead of {{ n(g4.batch1.tok_s_user, -1) }}, and a busy system delivers {{ x(RS.tokens_per_joule_best) }} the
   tokens per joule. At {{ n(I.headline.sla_tok_s_user) }} tok/s per user its decode cost is within 2x of the GPUs' either way (median {{ x(MC.cost_best.median, 2) }}). From
   {{ n(I.thresholds.rows[-1].tok_s_user_min, 0) }} tok/s per user up, where GPUs hold only a handful of users each, it is
   {{ x(I.thresholds.rows[-1].s.cost, 0) }} cheaper, at batch sizes where our GPU model is least certain.
2. **The memory architecture is the lever, and software comes first.** Better software on the same
   GPUs is worth {{ x(RG.speed_batch1) }} for a single user. A purpose-built chip that still keeps its weights in HBM
   adds no speed on top of that ({{ x(R["a_x4/g1_x4"].speed_batch1, 2) }}), but it is the cheapest whole system: {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.a_x4.cost_gain) }} lower cost
   than GPUs with prefill counted.
3. **It can do its own prefill, if prompts are pipelined.** On the published agentic workload prefill is
   {{ pct(I.end_to_end.rows.g0.prefill_share_of_cost) }} of what GPUs cost per output token. With {{ I.prefill.rows.s.prompts_in_flight }} prompts in flight the on-chip design
   prefills at {{ x(I.prefill.s_over_g0_cost, 2) }} the GPUs' cost per token; end to end it is {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s.cost_gain) }} lower in cost and {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s.tokens_per_joule_gain) }}
   better in tokens per joule than GPUs. Paired with HBM chips for prefill: {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s_decode_a_prefill.cost_gain) }} and {{ x(I.end_to_end.rows.s_decode_a_prefill.tokens_per_joule_gain) }}.
4. **Reloadable weights cost {{ x(I.designs.s_over_f.dies) }} the silicon.** In return: three die designs instead of
   {{ I.designs.f.cost.distinct_die_designs }}, and a new model version by loading weights instead of {{ usd(I.designs.f.cost.unique_mask_usd / 1e6, 0) }}M of new masks. The
   synthesized block shows that fixing a multiplier's weight saves only {{ x(SS.reloadable_over_fixed.hardwired_lane_area, 2) }} in that lane's area. The silicon
   difference is SRAM against mask-programmed storage, plus S's multiplier lanes ({{ pct(I.designs.s.area.mac_lanes / I.designs.s.area.silicon_mm2) }} of its silicon);
   the fixed design's density comes from one shipped chip with its arithmetic folded in. The fixed design's higher speed in our tables is not separable: the two designs' speeds come from two different published machines, not a property of reloadability.
5. **It needs scale.** {{ I.designs.s.area.dies }} dies, about {{ usd(I.designs.s.cost.total / 1e6, 1) }}M a system with its share of design cost.
   It breaks even with GPUs at about {{ n(I.demand.breakeven_tokens_per_day.s / 1e12, 1) }} trillion tokens a day of this one model.
6. **The gain narrows down the ladder for models shaped like this one.** We predicted the opposite.
   At about 8B parameters the speed gain is {{ x(I.ladder.rung_8b.gain.S.speed_batch1) }}. For dense models the published machines show
   it widening.

Our prior was a gain of about 2-10x. Speed and energy landed inside it; cost landed below it.
Two adversarial reviews changed these numbers substantially ([05](docs/05_limitations.md)): before
them the cost figure read {{ x(RV.headline_change["s/g0:cost_best"].before) if RV else "much higher" }}.

